{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"content":null,"reasoning":"We need answer in German, max ~700 words. Need parse problem. We are \"Ratssitz im Hause omegaflow\" with weird constraints. Need decide architecture questions. Must not invent facts. Numbers/quotes only from given evidence. Need output format.\n\nWe need reason carefully. Let's restate.\n\nWe have three open architecture questions. For each: verdict A/B/C(/D), 2-3 sentences justification, strongest counter/counter-evidence, if uncertainty explicitly pending + what to measure.\n\nImportant: no invented facts. Numbers and quotes only from given evidence. Answer German. Max ~700 words.\n\nNeed likely choose based on evidence. Let's analyze each.\n\nQuestion 1: R-2026-10-18-a: Where is receipt recorded in token ledger?\nCurrent state: token state (xfer) only in sender's ledger (Emitter). Receiver cannot acknowledge own receipt: `xfer close` on foreign token hard error. Receipt and self-completion both CLOSED — not distinguishable. Additionally `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks; `--fired` remains 0, completed reads as open. Earlier built `xfer ack` arm reverted (Commit eb2f1b749, −202 lines).\n\nOptions:\nA — own act `ACK <tok> by=<linie>.<sitz>` in receiver's ledger; ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED.\nB — as today: receiver writes CLOSED into emitter ledger.\nC — no new act: receipt state derived read-only from token ledger.\nD — one ledger per token instead of per line (both write same store).\n\nSecond question `--addressed` (independent): S1 hide closed blocks · S2 continue listing but with state column.\n\nNeed decide. Evidence: current receiver cannot close foreign token; receipt and completion both CLOSED indistinguishable; register_lookup --addressed lists closed blocks; --fired 0; completed reads as open. Earlier xfer ack arm reverted commit -202 lines. Need infer best.\n\nOption A gives distinguishable ACKED vs CLOSED, receiver owns receipt in own ledger. But maybe complexity? Option C read-only derivation avoids new act and ledger writes? But if state only in emitter ledger, receiver cannot quittieren? \"Quittung und Selbst-Erledigung sind beide CLOSED — nicht unterscheidbar.\" Could derive? Need know token ledger? Maybe C says no new act: receipt state derived read-only from token ledger. But if token ledger only emitter? Hmm.\n\nOption D one ledger per token both write same store: solves shared state but maybe concurrency/ownership? Not in evidence? Need not invent.\n\nOption B as today is broken (receiver cannot write emitter ledger? Actually \"Empfänger kann seinen eigenen Eingang nicht quittieren: xfer close auf fremden Token harter Fehler\" so B impossible? It says B wie heute: Empfänger schreibt CLOSED ins Emitter-Ledger. But evidence says cannot. So B likely not.\n\nA seems plausible: own ACK act in receiver ledger, distinguishable. But earlier xfer ack arm reverted; maybe reason? Need strongest riss: reverted commit -202 lines suggests prior ACK implementation failed/complex; maybe not enough evidence. Could be pending? Need decide.\n\nSecond question S1 vs S2. Evidence: `--addressed` lists closed blocks; `--fired` remains 0, completed reads as open. To avoid completed reads as open, either hide closed (S1) or list with state column (S2). Which is better? If hide, user asking \"what's open for me?\" sees only open; but maybe need audit? S2 preserves visibility and disambiguates. Evidence says \"Erledigtes liest sich als offen\" due no state. S2 directly fixes by state column. S1 hides but maybe loses trace. Need choose? The prompt asks \"Zweitfrage `--addressed` (unabhängig): S1 geschlossene Blöcke ausblenden · S2 weiter listen, aber mit Zustandsspalte.\" We need include in verdict maybe \"A + S2\" etc. Need decide.\n\nLet's parse exact requested output: \"F1 Verdikt: <A|B|C|D|pending> [+ S1|S2] — Begründung: … — Riss: …\" So verdict can include + S1/S2. Need choose.\n\nQuestion 2: Concurrency key register-coverage. Options A per-SHA + true; B per-ref + true (only latest run lives); C Debounce/Batch. Evidence: push on main fires 5-7 gates; last 100 runs = 25 different HEAD SHAs; in 10 minutes eight register-coverage runs, seven cancelled — in concurrency group new run deletes waiting predecessors, cancel-in-progress false protects only running. Gate checks HEAD state of register, no per-SHA artifact. Vorrunde 3:3 tie. A per-SHA (GLM, DeepSeek, Lumo: \"der HEAD ist je SHA definiert\") · B per-ref (Fugu, Mistral, Kimi: \"die Adresse des HEAD ist der Ref, per-SHA adressiert die Vergangenheit\"). Kimi practical: cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; gate must ignore cancelled.\n\nNeed decide. Let's reason.\n\nCurrent group `${{ github.ref }}` with false. Problem: multiple runs same ref queue; cancel-in-progress false protects running but new run cancels? Wait text: \"in einer Concurrency-Gruppe löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger, `cancel-in-progress: false` schützt nur den laufenden.\" Actually GitHub concurrency: if cancel-in-progress false, new run waits; if true, cancels running. \"löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger\" maybe when new run starts, pending runs in same group are cancelled? Need not.\n\nQuestion: should switch to group `${{ github.sha }}` with cancel-in-progress true (like ci-gate since 2026-10-08) instead of today ref false?\n\nEvidence gate checks HEAD state of register, no per-SHA artifact. If per-SHA group, each SHA gets own group; cancel-in-progress true only cancels same SHA runs (unlikely multiple same SHA) so no cancellation benefit; queue per SHA? Actually if group includes SHA, runs for different SHAs don't cancel each other, so many runs accumulate. Per-ref group means all runs on same ref share group; cancel-in-progress true cancels older running when new push? But if gate checks HEAD state, latest run should supersede older; per-ref true ensures only latest lives. That seems B. But A proponents say HEAD is defined per SHA. Need parse.\n\nOption A per-SHA + true: group by SHA. Since each push has unique SHA, no concurrency across pushes; cancel-in-progress true cancels duplicate runs for same SHA (rare). This doesn't solve 8 runs in 10 min? Actually each run different SHA, so no cancellation; queue grows. But gate checks HEAD state, not per-SHA artifact, so running old SHA after newer push is wasteful but maybe okay? Option B per-ref + true: all main runs same ref group; new run cancels running older, only latest lives. This matches \"nur der jüngste Lauf lebt\". Evidence says gate checks HEAD state, no per-SHA artifact, so per-ref is appropriate. Kimi practical cancel mid-write inconsistent; gate must ignore cancelled. But if cancel-in-progress true, mid-write cancellation could leave inconsistent state; need maybe not true? Option C debounce/batch could avoid. But question asks switch to per-SHA true vs today ref false; options include B per-ref true and C. Need decide.\n\nLet's examine wording: \"Soll `register-coverage.yml` auf `group: register-coverage-${{ github.sha }}` mit `cancel-in-progress: true` umgestellt werden (wie `ci-gate` seit 2026-10-08) statt heute `group: …-${{ github.ref }}` mit `false`?\" Then options: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true (nur der jüngste Lauf lebt) · C Debounce/Batch. So A is proposed change. B is alternative per-ref true. C debounce.\n\nEvidence: \"Das Gate prüft den HEAD-Zustand des Registers, kein je-SHA-Artefakt.\" This strongly favors B? If no per-SHA artifact, per-SHA grouping is wrong; per-ref grouping cancels older runs on same branch. But \"HEAD ist je SHA definiert\" maybe means HEAD commit SHA defines state, so per-SHA? Let's parse proponents:\nA per-SHA (GLM, DeepSeek, Lumo: „der HEAD ist je SHA definiert\") \nB per-ref (Fugu, Mistral, Kimi: „die Adresse des HEAD ist der Ref, per-SHA adressiert die Vergangenheit\").\nThese are philosophical. Evidence \"Gate prüft den HEAD-Zustand des Registers, kein je-SHA-Artefakt\" means it checks current HEAD, not artifact tied to SHA. If a run for old SHA completes after new push, it checks new HEAD? Actually workflow triggered by push; checkout SHA maybe old? Let's think GitHub: push to main triggers workflow on that commit. It checks out that commit. Register state at that commit. If later push, old run still checks old commit's register, not HEAD. But \"HEAD-Zustand des Registers\" maybe means register's HEAD (latest) not per commit? Hmm.\n\nThe phrase \"kein je-SHA-Artefakt\" means no artifact per SHA, so no need to preserve old runs. Thus cancel older runs is okay. Per-ref true cancels older runs on same ref. Per-SHA true does not cancel across SHAs, so doesn't solve. So B likely.\n\nBut Kimi practical: cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; gate must ignore cancelled. This is a riss for B (cancel-in-progress true). Could choose C debounce/batch to avoid mid-write cancellation? But options? Need decide based on evidence. The question asks whether to switch to per-SHA true. Given tie 3:3, evidence maybe supports B not A. But is B safe? \"cancel-in","reasoning_content":"We need answer in German, max ~700 words. Need parse problem. We are \"Ratssitz im Hause omegaflow\" with weird constraints. Need decide architecture questions. Must not invent facts. Numbers/quotes only from given evidence. Need output format.\n\nWe need reason carefully. Let's restate.\n\nWe have three open architecture questions. For each: verdict A/B/C(/D), 2-3 sentences justification, strongest counter/counter-evidence, if uncertainty explicitly pending + what to measure.\n\nImportant: no invented facts. Numbers and quotes only from given evidence. Answer German. Max ~700 words.\n\nNeed likely choose based on evidence. Let's analyze each.\n\nQuestion 1: R-2026-10-18-a: Where is receipt recorded in token ledger?\nCurrent state: token state (xfer) only in sender's ledger (Emitter). Receiver cannot acknowledge own receipt: `xfer close` on foreign token hard error. 